(2113417, 284616, 5885, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Greetings from Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, as requested, here is an indepth critique of your submission.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Wait! This score what? Nooo.. there is no way! I really admired this shot and gave it a high score. Wow! I''m just speechless at that score.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>It could be stronger, but it''s not at all bad. I like to see the crack in the ice run diagonally across the photo pulling the eye from top to bottom. Leading the eye is a strong tool. You force people to look at what you want them to look at.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour, focus, and light):</b> <br/> <br/>Focus - it''s very nice and sharp on the ice hanging below. I''d like to see a bit deeper DoF so that the snow above were a bit sharper.<br/> <br/>Colour - Not a lot of use of colour, but this photo didn''t need it. Your whites are white however, so this tells me you got WB correct.<br/> <br/>Light - A bit overexposed on the top part of the photo. But, going darker you risk loosing detail in the crack. <br/>Compromise? Not sure if you shot this in JPEG or RAW, but in RAW you may have been able to have the best of both worlds, by processing two seperate TIFFs from one raw file. One would be overexposed and one would be underexposed. You then use the best parts of both files to merge a single image with deeper tonal range.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Not sure all the voters saw this as fitting the challenge. I do, but I''m also not very strict on what I see as fitting the challenge. But, to get the high scores, you have to try to please as many people as you can.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>It''s a good photo. Needs a little bit of work on the technicals, but it''s not by any means bad. Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>Hope to see more from you soon,<br/>Leroy</td>', '::: Greetings from Critique Club :::  Hi, as requested, here is an indepth critique of your submission. First Impression - the most important one: Wait! This score what? Nooo.. there is no way! I really admired this shot and gave it a high score. Wow! I''m just speechless at that score. Composition: It could be stronger, but it''s not at all bad. I like to see the crack in the ice run diagonally across the photo pulling the eye from top to bottom. Leading the eye is a strong tool. You force people to look at what you want them to look at. Technical (Colour, focus, and light):  Focus - it''s very nice and sharp on the ice hanging below. I''d like to see a bit deeper DoF so that the snow above were a bit sharper. Colour - Not a lot of use of colour, but this photo didn''t need it. Your whites are white however, so this tells me you got WB correct. Light - A bit overexposed on the top part of the photo. But, going darker you risk loosing detail in the crack. Compromise? Not sure if you shot this in JPEG or RAW, but in RAW you may have been able to have the best of both worlds, by processing two seperate TIFFs from one raw file. One would be overexposed and one would be underexposed. You then use the best parts of both files to merge a single image with deeper tonal range. To grow its vote?: Not sure all the voters saw this as fitting the challenge. I do, but I''m also not very strict on what I see as fitting the challenge. But, to get the high scores, you have to try to please as many people as you can. Summary: It''s a good photo. Needs a little bit of work on the technicals, but it''s not by any means bad. Keep up the good work. Hope to see more from you soon,Leroy', '2006-02-02 01:33:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2113574, 284783, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Your photographers comments do not give me much of an idea of what you were trying to accomplish, so let''s see what we can come up with...<br/> <br/>You have some very nice tones in this shot.  Unfortunately because of how dark the shot is you don''t have much OF the tones to give the shot a lot of feeling.<br/> <br/>My eye keeps trying to find a place to rest.  An actual subject of the photo.  The house itself doesn''t have enough ooommpf to hold the attention all by itself so my eye keeps wandering.  It tries to land on the sign in the center of the shot.  I can''t read what the sign says though due to the sizing constraints of the medium that is DPC.<br/> <br/>That is part of the problem with this shot.  At 8x10 this may look very nice.  At 640xSomething it just doesn''t have enough attention grabbing power.  This is something to keep in mind when entering challenges at this site.<br/> <br/>I think you have a good eye and I think that this probably is much more interesting at a decent print size but in the challenge situation that you placed it in, it didn''t have a chance...<br/> <br/>Hope this makes sense and is helpfull.<br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: Your photographers comments do not give me much of an idea of what you were trying to accomplish, so let''s see what we can come up with... You have some very nice tones in this shot.  Unfortunately because of how dark the shot is you don''t have much OF the tones to give the shot a lot of feeling. My eye keeps trying to find a place to rest.  An actual subject of the photo.  The house itself doesn''t have enough ooommpf to hold the attention all by itself so my eye keeps wandering.  It tries to land on the sign in the center of the shot.  I can''t read what the sign says though due to the sizing constraints of the medium that is DPC. That is part of the problem with this shot.  At 8x10 this may look very nice.  At 640xSomething it just doesn''t have enough attention grabbing power.  This is something to keep in mind when entering challenges at this site. I think you have a good eye and I think that this probably is much more interesting at a decent print size but in the challenge situation that you placed it in, it didn''t have a chance... Hope this makes sense and is helpfull.YoursTC', '2006-02-02 03:44:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(2113581, 282241, 30807, '<td valign="top">What amazing eyes you have - Large, Clear, Wonderful Colour - If you are not a model, you should be. Trust me<br/> <br/>The Photo is great too - Very nicely exposed, very crisp where needed, I look forward to seeing more of your work. An inspiration to me. Thank you very much</td>', 'What amazing eyes you have - Large, Clear, Wonderful Colour - If you are not a model, you should be. Trust me The Photo is great too - Very nicely exposed, very crisp where needed, I look forward to seeing more of your work. An inspiration to me. Thank you very much', '2006-02-02 03:56:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115092, 284566, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Nice, dreamy image of an island gettaway with it''s own pier.  And the voters were pretty good to you with that 5.5.  <br/> <br/>I''ve got just one suggestion for you and if the topography isn''t right for it, well, that''s the way it goes:  the island and the mainland behind it merge drastically.  Could you get either a lot higher to give them separation or get a lot lower to eliminate the mainland all together.  As I say, it''s just a thought.  And if you would need a skyhook, well "my bad" for suggesting it...<br/>;&gt;)<br/> <br/>In any event,keep up the good work and I''ll look forward to seeing more of your images in DPChallenge.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Nice, dreamy image of an island gettaway with it''s own pier.  And the voters were pretty good to you with that 5.5.   I''ve got just one suggestion for you and if the topography isn''t right for it, well, that''s the way it goes:  the island and the mainland behind it merge drastically.  Could you get either a lot higher to give them separation or get a lot lower to eliminate the mainland all together.  As I say, it''s just a thought.  And if you would need a skyhook, well "my bad" for suggesting it...;>) In any event,keep up the good work and I''ll look forward to seeing more of your images in DPChallenge. ', '2006-02-02 15:21:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115110, 284077, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>I usually try to figure out which challenge a CC photo came from before I look to see which one it is.  It helps me to determine how the photo fits into the challenge.   I really thought this was "Abstract", although I think we may still be voting on that.<br/> <br/>As an abstract, I do like this very much.   The diagonals on the right, the feel of motion, the smoothness of the "wave" in areas...along with the colors - all really work well together for a pleasing shot.  I would probably have cropped just slightly more from the top.   The small curl of wave, just above the darker spot (which is just above the reflecting area) has just the top portion cut off, which I find a bit distracting. I would have cropped just above the dark area, as I think it brings the eye more to the central part of the photo.<br/> <br/>Technically,  I think the blur really does work here.  It feels more like motion to me, less like out of focus and I think that is an important distinction.  Exposure seems excellent.  Some might think a bit dark, but I think the details are there and I personally like the darker shades to the shot.<br/> <br/>For the fantasy challenge, I don''t believe there is a strong connection.  I wouldn''t go so far as to say it doesn''t meet the challenge - but I wouldn''t give extra points for a strong connection.  If I voted, I probably would have given this shot a 7.  <br/> <br/>Hope this is helpful!<br/>Judy</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club. I usually try to figure out which challenge a CC photo came from before I look to see which one it is.  It helps me to determine how the photo fits into the challenge.   I really thought this was "Abstract", although I think we may still be voting on that. As an abstract, I do like this very much.   The diagonals on the right, the feel of motion, the smoothness of the "wave" in areas...along with the colors - all really work well together for a pleasing shot.  I would probably have cropped just slightly more from the top.   The small curl of wave, just above the darker spot (which is just above the reflecting area) has just the top portion cut off, which I find a bit distracting. I would have cropped just above the dark area, as I think it brings the eye more to the central part of the photo. Technically,  I think the blur really does work here.  It feels more like motion to me, less like out of focus and I think that is an important distinction.  Exposure seems excellent.  Some might think a bit dark, but I think the details are there and I personally like the darker shades to the shot. For the fantasy challenge, I don''t believe there is a strong connection.  I wouldn''t go so far as to say it doesn''t meet the challenge - but I wouldn''t give extra points for a strong connection.  If I voted, I probably would have given this shot a 7.   Hope this is helpful!Judy', '2006-02-02 15:27:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115146, 284762, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>What a cute shot!  Technically the focus and lighting are fine, if not very creative.  Obviously, you''ve caught kitty in a fun pose.  I usually try to determine which challenge a critque photo has come from, before I actually look to see which challenge.  I had no clue with this one, other than possibly the "Best of 2005" challenge.   It does not convey fantasy to me at all.  I suppose, given the title it could fit, but I would not give any extra points at all for meeting the challenge well.  While I wouldn''t deduct points (I''m pretty liberal with the challenge interpretations and give the photographer the benefit of the doubt) I think your score probably did suffer froma poor connection to the theme.<br/> <br/>As it is,  it''s a cute kitty shot but not unique enough or creative enough to carry a wow factor for the viewer who doesn''t have a connection with this particular cat.  There is nothing technically I think you should change about the shot.  I like how the eyes are looking right at the camera.  I didn''t not vote in this challenge, but if I had voted, I would have given your kitty shot a 5.  <br/> <br/>I hope this is helpful.<br/> <br/>Judy</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! What a cute shot!  Technically the focus and lighting are fine, if not very creative.  Obviously, you''ve caught kitty in a fun pose.  I usually try to determine which challenge a critque photo has come from, before I actually look to see which challenge.  I had no clue with this one, other than possibly the "Best of 2005" challenge.   It does not convey fantasy to me at all.  I suppose, given the title it could fit, but I would not give any extra points at all for meeting the challenge well.  While I wouldn''t deduct points (I''m pretty liberal with the challenge interpretations and give the photographer the benefit of the doubt) I think your score probably did suffer froma poor connection to the theme. As it is,  it''s a cute kitty shot but not unique enough or creative enough to carry a wow factor for the viewer who doesn''t have a connection with this particular cat.  There is nothing technically I think you should change about the shot.  I like how the eyes are looking right at the camera.  I didn''t not vote in this challenge, but if I had voted, I would have given your kitty shot a 5.   I hope this is helpful. Judy', '2006-02-02 15:37:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115288, 284628, 5728, '<td valign="top">Nice idea, but I think the ground should have been kept as one and not cloned into parts, it takes away from the overall impact.</td>', 'Nice idea, but I think the ground should have been kept as one and not cloned into parts, it takes away from the overall impact.', '2006-02-02 16:30:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115321, 284821, 5728, '<td valign="top">Hey Robert, I am doing my one and only yearly CC comment and I got your photo :)<br/> <br/>Congrats on a top placing with this image, 12th is nothing to complain about! But there are a few issues I''d like to touch on with your image. I like the detail in both the petals and the eye although there is a slight more softness in the petals compared to the eye which is sharper. I myself would have gone for a compromise and sharpened the petals a bit and blurred the eye a touch to meet somewhere in the middle. But only ever so slightly. The lighting and composition is very good, nothing to push there at all. Only two more little issues, the black hairs in the top right should be cloned away, they are only disturbing there. And secondly the right part of the eye is too closed up for me, I would have worked more of the eye into the image to see more of the full eye so that most of the outline is present, as it is the main focal point in your photo.<br/> <br/>Other than that, a super entry, good processing and one of the better photos I have seen from you.<br/> <br/>Gary</td>', 'Hey Robert, I am doing my one and only yearly CC comment and I got your photo :) Congrats on a top placing with this image, 12th is nothing to complain about! But there are a few issues I''d like to touch on with your image. I like the detail in both the petals and the eye although there is a slight more softness in the petals compared to the eye which is sharper. I myself would have gone for a compromise and sharpened the petals a bit and blurred the eye a touch to meet somewhere in the middle. But only ever so slightly. The lighting and composition is very good, nothing to push there at all. Only two more little issues, the black hairs in the top right should be cloned away, they are only disturbing there. And secondly the right part of the eye is too closed up for me, I would have worked more of the eye into the image to see more of the full eye so that most of the outline is present, as it is the main focal point in your photo. Other than that, a super entry, good processing and one of the better photos I have seen from you. Gary', '2006-02-02 16:45:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115432, 283282, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>You have a charming image that scored exceptionally well in the Fantasy Challenge.  It certainly is one you can be proud of and if there wasn''t such fierce competition, you would probably have another ribbon on your Profile Page.<br/> <br/>So, I''m going to end my "critique" here and go on to the next person on our very long list.<br/> <br/>My best wishes for your continued success in DPC.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  You have a charming image that scored exceptionally well in the Fantasy Challenge.  It certainly is one you can be proud of and if there wasn''t such fierce competition, you would probably have another ribbon on your Profile Page. So, I''m going to end my "critique" here and go on to the next person on our very long list. My best wishes for your continued success in DPC. Alice', '2006-02-02 17:31:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2115850, 282894, 42013, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>First impression: well composed, with an interesting pan effect.<br/> <br/>Composition: Good placement of subject, might have been strengthened by cropping out just a little more of the foreground.  Blue color is nice and the way the angle of the road slopes with the direction of travel as it gives the picture a nice feeling of motion(in addition to the panning technique).  <br/> <br/>Technical: Great job on the panning, your skill should work well in the current open challenge.<br/> <br/>Score/Challenge result: 5.2xx is a very respectable score and I suspect that in a fantasy challenge many voters didn''t give you enough credit for the panning technique you used.  Also, along those same lines, many voters might feel that a car is not interesting enough to give a high vote.  So, bottom line...your technique makes a potentially uninteresting subject interesting.<br/> <br/>And...Make sure you enter something in the panning motion challenge this week :)<br/> <br/>any questions?? just PM me.<br/> <br/>Mark<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club... First impression: well composed, with an interesting pan effect. Composition: Good placement of subject, might have been strengthened by cropping out just a little more of the foreground.  Blue color is nice and the way the angle of the road slopes with the direction of travel as it gives the picture a nice feeling of motion(in addition to the panning technique).   Technical: Great job on the panning, your skill should work well in the current open challenge. Score/Challenge result: 5.2xx is a very respectable score and I suspect that in a fantasy challenge many voters didn''t give you enough credit for the panning technique you used.  Also, along those same lines, many voters might feel that a car is not interesting enough to give a high vote.  So, bottom line...your technique makes a potentially uninteresting subject interesting. And...Make sure you enter something in the panning motion challenge this week :) any questions?? just PM me. Mark ', '2006-02-02 19:48:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2116886, 284854, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>This is one hot car ... I love the colours and the impression made not only by the car but by the flooring as well.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>This has been done very well, you have managed to capture the vehicle without any major flashpoints ... and it is well positioned in the picutre.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Defiantely meets my idea of a fantasy.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>fantastic lighting .. and great colours ... I like the effects of the blending on the background.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Hard to know really, you scored well above average and it is a good picture.   In fact there are only around three picutres in the top 25 that do not have people ... no I am not suggesting a blond chick accross the bonnet though.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Great shot... fantastic post processing.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: This is one hot car ... I love the colours and the impression made not only by the car but by the flooring as well. Composition: This has been done very well, you have managed to capture the vehicle without any major flashpoints ... and it is well positioned in the picutre. Subject: Defiantely meets my idea of a fantasy. Technical (Colour and light): fantastic lighting .. and great colours ... I like the effects of the blending on the background. To grow its vote?: Hard to know really, you scored well above average and it is a good picture.   In fact there are only around three picutres in the top 25 that do not have people ... no I am not suggesting a blond chick accross the bonnet though. Summary: Great shot... fantastic post processing. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-02-03 01:02:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2116903, 284770, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Emotions are running wild ... this has such a wonderful feeling about it and is so very real ... did the powers in the wand not work?<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>And you were the model as well ...this totally blew me away ... as many voters said the tilted floor works .. the colours on the wall are a great complement to the "pink" hair .. the wings look like they are actually on your back.  and it almost perfectly hits thirds.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Defiantely meets my idea of a fantasy.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>well lit and well coloured .. .adding the pink hair was genuis ...<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Top 10 ... Be sooo proud of that .. you had stiff competition and took most of them apart.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Fantastic shot ... it is well balanced and just totally wistful.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Emotions are running wild ... this has such a wonderful feeling about it and is so very real ... did the powers in the wand not work? Composition: And you were the model as well ...this totally blew me away ... as many voters said the tilted floor works .. the colours on the wall are a great complement to the "pink" hair .. the wings look like they are actually on your back.  and it almost perfectly hits thirds. Subject: Defiantely meets my idea of a fantasy. Technical (Colour and light): well lit and well coloured .. .adding the pink hair was genuis ... To grow its vote?: Top 10 ... Be sooo proud of that .. you had stiff competition and took most of them apart. Summary: Fantastic shot ... it is well balanced and just totally wistful. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-02-03 01:10:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(2117112, 282220, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Your photographers comments do not give me much to go on as to what you were trying to do with this shot but let''s see what we can come up with.<br/> <br/>This shot begs for me to tell you what I like and don''t like so I''ll use that format for this critique...<br/> <br/>What I like:  First of all, I am a HUGE fan of the soft focus, especially in portraiture.  If anyone says this is OOF they don''t understand soft focus.  All the detail is there.  Just look at the eyelashes!  The wrinkle under your right eye.  The creases in your lips.  The detail is there.  It''s just soft.  Love that effect!  Second, I am also a big fan of high key.  It''s very hard to pull this off in a portrait and not make it look blown out.  You did this perfectly.  I would point out the same details in my first point.  Thirdly,  I am very happy with the fact that you left in what many models (mostly women) would consider imperfections.  Thank you for not neatimaging your blemishes or freckles from your cheek.  They give you character and make your face what it is.  Lastly...  You have great catch lights in the eyes!  They make a portrait.  Without them a model tends to look like a zombie...  Ooops, I almost forgot.  I love the tonality that this shot has.  It''s partly due to the high key effect, but it''s not all white as some shots of this genre tend toward.<br/> <br/>What I don''t like:  This is a tad bit harder for this shot as there is so much that I like.  Your cropping from top to bottom is perfect.  You have all the detail that you need to put your personality into the shot.  However I''m a little put off by the cropping from left to right.  The way you cut your left eye (right in the image) just doesn''t look right to me.  I don''t know why but that''s the way I see it.  The shot appears to be a little bit crooked.  I don''t know if this is just the way your face is or if it is a trick of the camera angle.  If you rotated the top a tad to the left it would look straighter.  If you rotated the top to the right a little it would look artsy.  The way you have it looks like an oversight.  You have what looks like a hot pixel in your mouth.  My eye keeps getting drawn to that spot.  I keep wondering if you are chewing on something (just kidding) or if it''s just a bad reflection.  More detail there would be good but with just one tiny little point of light it looks wrong.<br/> <br/>Good vs Bad?  You have done an aweseome job with this shot and the good points far outweigh the bad...  Work on some of the things I don''t like and this would move from a very cool shot to one that is great.  Why did this not score better?  I would have to say it is a combination of the soft focus (which I love but DPCer''s as a whole frown upon) and the cropping left to right.  I really wish that soft focus shots would do better here.  I beg you to keep posting these kinds of shots if that is what you like to shoot and maybe we can change the voting patterns of the masses.<br/>Kudos on what I think is a great image,<br/>TC<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-03 04:37:32.</i></td>', 'From the Critique Club... Your photographers comments do not give me much to go on as to what you were trying to do with this shot but let''s see what we can come up with. This shot begs for me to tell you what I like and don''t like so I''ll use that format for this critique... What I like:  First of all, I am a HUGE fan of the soft focus, especially in portraiture.  If anyone says this is OOF they don''t understand soft focus.  All the detail is there.  Just look at the eyelashes!  The wrinkle under your right eye.  The creases in your lips.  The detail is there.  It''s just soft.  Love that effect!  Second, I am also a big fan of high key.  It''s very hard to pull this off in a portrait and not make it look blown out.  You did this perfectly.  I would point out the same details in my first p