(2117364, 283632, 37430, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club! This is my second ever Critique Club comment and I''d never expected to get a picture from Manny to critique :) Congratulation on n''th well deserved ribbon!<br/> <br/>My standard text :)<br/>-------------------<br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. <br/> <br/>Composition :<br/>-------------<br/>Perfect!!!! Do I need to say more? This is great photo which really fits the challenge. The "feel" of being in fantasy land is what it communicates. I dont want to embarrass myself by leaving any comments which might sound silly (for such perfect photo) :)<br/> <br/>Camera Work :<br/>-------------<br/> <br/>Perfect. Period. <br/> <br/>Post-processing :<br/>-----------------<br/> <br/>The idea of sharpening to enhance the pattern on dress worked very well. I dont know if a little more contrast would have been helpful for those who have their monitors on brighter side. Diffused glow really makes this from fairy land :)<br/> <br/>Summary :<br/>---------<br/>Perfect picture. I did not voted for this challenge but would have voted 9 for this one (because 9 is the best I ever give :)) but I could have guessed it to be your submission. It really deserved to be in top 3 ... and I feel it should have been at top. But thats because of my personal preferece for pictures which look less of "digital art". Great work!<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT)<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/>-Tej<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is my second ever Critique Club comment and I''d never expected to get a picture from Manny to critique :) Congratulation on n''th well deserved ribbon! My standard text :)-------------------I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions.  Composition :-------------Perfect!!!! Do I need to say more? This is great photo which really fits the challenge. The "feel" of being in fantasy land is what it communicates. I dont want to embarrass myself by leaving any comments which might sound silly (for such perfect photo) :) Camera Work :------------- Perfect. Period.  Post-processing :----------------- The idea of sharpening to enhance the pattern on dress worked very well. I dont know if a little more contrast would have been helpful for those who have their monitors on brighter side. Diffused glow really makes this from fairy land :) Summary :---------Perfect picture. I did not voted for this challenge but would have voted 9 for this one (because 9 is the best I ever give :)) but I could have guessed it to be your submission. It really deserved to be in top 3 ... and I feel it should have been at top. But thats because of my personal preferece for pictures which look less of "digital art". Great work! If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT) Wish you a great day!-Tej ', '2006-02-03 08:15:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(2117994, 284239, 44107, '<td valign="top">Great shot!  Beautiful girl.</td>', 'Great shot!  Beautiful girl.', '2006-02-03 11:08:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118115, 284552, 9073, '<td valign="top">***Greetings from the critique club***<br/> <br/>(this section is written prior to reading the existing comments...)<br/>This is a tough one. I''m not immediately drawn to this photo, so my task it to explain why.<br/> <br/>The photo is technically very well done. You have achieved a wonderful softness in the back that matches the mood of your title perfectly. I must confess that I''m not very familiar with the Disney Fantasia, although I''ve come across a great many renaissance ones in my time. For this analysis, therefore, I''ll have to look at the elements in your frame to get the meaning. What I see immediately is a woman on a swing which is flower-garland decorated. Some garland leaves have fallen, or are sprinkled, around the swing. The swing and the attached tree are surrounded by a circle of stones. There is a dalmation inside the ring. A winged angel is placed against the tree. It is small.<br/> <br/>The title of fantasia resonates in the woman''s head. Inside this stone ring, she is no longer grown up. She is transported back to her childhood. Maybe the dog is now dead, a childhood pet forever loved. The tree deserves some mention. Its majesty and strength may symbolise the saftey found within the circle, or it might relate to a particular place the swing was in, or both. The expression on the face is hard to make out, but it doesn''t appear troubled (unlike the dog''s). The pure white dress helps emphasise the purity of the girl, or it could represent some religious meaning. The angel also may be read in two ways: the obvious relgious overtone, or in the sense of ''daddy''s little angel''. Being a fantasia for one''s youth, the latter interpretation feels more secure. <br/> <br/>(You can tell that I''m trying to make sense of the elements of this photo.)<br/> <br/>There are other elements which bear more on photographic and artistic choice than on intellectual placing of ideas within a frame. <br/> <br/>The first of these is the colour scheme. Browns dominate the lower section and greens the upper. Neither colour class are presented aggressively. At the very top of the frame, a blue white section appears. This white motive reappears in the dalmation, in the stones and in the dress. The colour scheme is developed convincingly in the composition. The split between the green and the brown area is roughly at the mid-point, with the brown being extended into the green linking the two areas. The long branches just into the green tie the lower brown into both the top and to the frame border. The white permeates both main colour areas and provides its own strong graphic content.<br/> <br/>The second element is the actual composition of the elements. The mid-point split has already been mentioned. The tree springs from the centre upwards and outwards. The woman is more or less in the centre. Between her and the dog is the angel. I can''t make sense of the placing of these latter two elements. The stone circle dominates the foreground. <br/> <br/>So far, I''ve only tried to describe what I actually see in the photo, not what I feel about it. The over-riding feeling I get when I look at it again and again is that I *should* be impressed by the intelligence and complexity of the shot. There are two reasons why I don''t. The first is technical, and the second personal.<br/> <br/>Technically, I find the shot messy. I could be wrong, but the blurring on the shrubs on the left is stronger than that on the right. At the same focal distance, the detail in both sides should be the same. It''s not. The same blurring problem (as I label it) affects the overhanging branch in the top right section. The dog''s tail joins with the stone ring, blurring the distinction between the two. The swinging girl isn''t clearly distinguished from the tree which muddies the sensation of what is really the subject. (If this is deliberate, please forgive me: there is the exact same loss of distinction between the left foot of the woman and the angel.) The garland-decorated swing is partially lost.<br/> <br/>However, the most messy aspect of the shot is the feeling that the concept is messy. Fair enough, it''s valid to just put together a collection of elements of the perfect childhood, you might say. But that collection itself comes across as messy. To be frank, I''d like either a) much more, far more items for a pot-pourri, or b) a focussed shot on a particular aspect of the fantasia. This shot gives me neither. Too much might ruin a photo, but not enough weakens it. Choose your argument.<br/> <br/>A minor point on paper, but large in practice, is the messiness of the colour scheme. It has been orchestrated very well, but the actual choice of hues, I feel, is weak. This leads to my second, more personal, point.<br/> <br/>I''m not a woman. (You''ll see that in my profile page.) I don''t relate well to female images of femininity. I can understand them, I believe. But the colour choices of, say, my wife, leave me cold. I''m not religious at all. When I see religious iconography, I''m unsure as to what to think about the owner. There are so many reasons for religion that I''m unwilling to arbitarily assign one to an image I may encounter. For these two reasons, I suppose, this photo does not attract me. But hey, we''re all different. Now, I''m going to read the comments left on this photo and see if I can add anything...<br/> <br/>Just one point: I thought that the swing should be moving at first, but after consideration, realised that contemplation usually occurs quietly. Then I understood that this scene is reflective, looking back, not a reconstruction of what actually had happened. Unmoving swing, as you have it, is better.<br/> <br/>My final comments are just random thoughts that occurred to me during this cc process.<br/> <br/>1. I really like your thought process as it shows itself in this photo.<br/>2. Have all the actors in unison unless you have a reason not to. The woman looks up, the dog and the statue don''t.<br/>3. The elements are challenging. Why isn''t the composition?<br/>4. The sky looks unintentional.<br/>5. I''d have preferred the garland to be very different from the backing tree.<br/>6. I wish that there had been a visual clue to how the elements relate within the frame. I suppose that this is my biggest quandary. On this note, I''ll stop.<br/> <br/>If you have any comments on my comments, please feel to chew the cud. <br/> <br/>Best wishes,<br/> <br/>Jim<br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-04 02:47:42.</i></td>', '***Greetings from the critique club*** (this section is written prior to reading the existing comments...)This is a tough one. I''m not immediately drawn to this photo, so my task it to explain why. The photo is technically very well done. You have achieved a wonderful softness in the back that matches the mood of your title perfectly. I must confess that I''m not very familiar with the Disney Fantasia, although I''ve come across a great many renaissance ones in my time. For this analysis, therefore, I''ll have to look at the elements in your frame to get the meaning. What I see immediately is a woman on a swing which is flower-garland decorated. Some garland leaves have fallen, or are sprinkled, around the swing. The swing and the attached tree are surrounded by a circle of stones. There is a dalmation inside the ring. A winged angel is placed against the tree. It is small. The title of fantasia resonates in the woman''s head. Inside this stone ring, she is no longer grown up. She is transported back to her childhood. Maybe the dog is now dead, a childhood pet forever loved. The tree deserves some mention. Its majesty and strength may symbolise the saftey found within the circle, or it might relate to a particular place the swing was in, or both. The expression on the face is hard to make out, but it doesn''t appear troubled (unlike the dog''s). The pure white dress helps emphasise the purity of the girl, or it could represent some religious meaning. The angel also may be read in two ways: the obvious relgious overtone, or in the sense of ''daddy''s little angel''. Being a fantasia for one''s youth, the latter interpretation feels more secure.  (You can tell that I''m trying to make sense of the elements of this photo.) There are other elements which bear more on photographic and artistic choice than on intellectual placing of ideas within a frame.  The first of these is the colour scheme. Browns dominate the lower section and greens the upper. Neither colour class are presented aggressively. At the very top of the frame, a blue white section appears. This white motive reappears in the dalmation, in the stones and in the dress. The colour scheme is developed convincingly in the composition. The split between the green and the brown area is roughly at the mid-point, with the brown being extended into the green linking the two areas. The long branches just into the green tie the lower brown into both the top and to the frame border. The white permeates both main colour areas and provides its own strong graphic content. The second element is the actual composition of the elements. The mid-point split has already been mentioned. The tree springs from the centre upwards and outwards. The woman is more or less in the centre. Between her and the dog is the angel. I can''t make sense of the placing of these latter two elements. The stone circle dominates the foreground.  So far, I''ve only tried to describe what I actually see in the photo, not what I feel about it. The over-riding feeling I get when I look at it again and again is that I *should* be impressed by the intelligence and complexity of the shot. There are two reasons why I don''t. The first is technical, and the second personal. Technically, I find the shot messy. I could be wrong, but the blurring on the shrubs on the left is stronger than that on the right. At the same focal distance, the detail in both sides should be the same. It''s not. The same blurring problem (as I label it) affects the overhanging branch in the top right section. The dog''s tail joins with the stone ring, blurring the distinction between the two. The swinging girl isn''t clearly distinguished from the tree which muddies the sensation of what is really the subject. (If this is deliberate, please forgive me: there is the exact same loss of distinction between the left foot of the woman and the angel.) The garland-decorated swing is partially lost. However, the most messy aspect of the shot is the feeling that the concept is messy. Fair enough, it''s valid to just put together a collection of elements of the perfect childhood, you might say. But that collection itself comes across as messy. To be frank, I''d like either a) much more, far more items for a pot-pourri, or b) a focussed shot on a particular aspect of the fantasia. This shot gives me neither. Too much might ruin a photo, but not enough weakens it. Choose your argument. A minor point on paper, but large in practice, is the messiness of the colour scheme. It has been orchestrated very well, but the actual choice of hues, I feel, is weak. This leads to my second, more personal, point. I''m not a woman. (You''ll see that in my profile page.) I don''t relate well to female images of femininity. I can understand them, I believe. But the colour choices of, say, my wife, leave me cold. I''m not religious at all. When I see religious iconography, I''m unsure as to what to think about the owner. There are so many reasons for religion that I''m unwilling to arbitarily assign one to an image I may encounter. For these two reasons, I suppose, this photo does not attract me. But hey, we''re all different. Now, I''m going to read the comments left on this photo and see if I can add anything... Just one point: I thought that the swing should be moving at first, but after consideration, realised that contemplation usually occurs quietly. Then I understood that this scene is reflective, looking back, not a reconstruction of what actually had happened. Unmoving swing, as you have it, is better. My final comments are just random thoughts that occurred to me during this cc process. 1. I really like your thought process as it shows itself in this photo.2. Have all the actors in unison unless you have a reason not to. The woman looks up, the dog and the statue don''t.3. The elements are challenging. Why isn''t the composition?4. The sky looks unintentional.5. I''d have preferred the garland to be very different from the backing tree.6. I wish that there had been a visual clue to how the elements relate within the frame. I suppose that this is my biggest quandary. On this note, I''ll stop. If you have any comments on my comments, please feel to chew the cud.  Best wishes, Jim', '2006-02-03 11:40:41.000000', '2006-02-04 02:47:42.000000', 0),
(2118172, 284217, 9073, '<td valign="top">I can only imagine that you scored so lowly in this challege because folks didn''t think you hit the challenge. It''s a great photo. Congratulations.</td>', 'I can only imagine that you scored so lowly in this challege because folks didn''t think you hit the challenge. It''s a great photo. Congratulations.', '2006-02-03 12:05:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118176, 284866, 9073, '<td valign="top">I''d hate to meet you, if this is what you''re really like...<br/> <br/>Technically, the PS work on the ears could have been more refined.<br/> <br/>Jim</td>', 'I''d hate to meet you, if this is what you''re really like... Technically, the PS work on the ears could have been more refined. Jim', '2006-02-03 12:07:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118189, 284698, 21816, '<td valign="top"><b>*Greetings from the Critique Club*</b> <br/> <br/>This is a great little photo. The idea is so different.<br/> <br/>The main thing that hits me here is that the subject is so dead on and from the front. I keep thinking of it from a slightly different angle and keep trying to picture the impact of it.<br/> <br/>I like the lighting you have achieved here and it works very well with the colours. I''d be curious to know what you used for lighting. You''ve done well with the saturation as to not over do it. It sits very nicely and isn''t overpowering. I think this, along with the old style sewing machine, adds a nice ''yesteryear'' type charm to the photo.<br/> <br/>The depth of field and crispness help finish this off and there''s some nice detail in the wings. Everything sits well from front to back. <br/> <br/>Well done with the votes this one received and it''s placement. Both your entries to date have performed well and best of luck with future challenges.<br/> <br/>Neil</td>', '*Greetings from the Critique Club*  This is a great little photo. The idea is so different. The main thing that hits me here is that the subject is so dead on and from the front. I keep thinking of it from a slightly different angle and keep trying to picture the impact of it. I like the lighting you have achieved here and it works very well with the colours. I''d be curious to know what you used for lighting. You''ve done well with the saturation as to not over do it. It sits very nicely and isn''t overpowering. I think this, along with the old style sewing machine, adds a nice ''yesteryear'' type charm to the photo. The depth of field and crispness help finish this off and there''s some nice detail in the wings. Everything sits well from front to back.  Well done with the votes this one received and it''s placement. Both your entries to date have performed well and best of luck with future challenges. Neil', '2006-02-03 12:11:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118221, 284779, 9073, '<td valign="top">***greetings from the critique club***<br/> <br/>(I''m writing this before I read the comments and score...)<br/> <br/>These are thoughts as they occur to me.<br/> <br/>#1 Good strong lines. Is this wise given the theme of fantasy?<br/>#2 The overall darkness does not lend itself to a fantastic approach unless you wish to appear devilish.<br/>#3 The vinegetting is unfortunate.<br/>#4 More toning would fit the DPC style better: blacker darks and brighter whites.<br/>#5 Great lines (did I already say that?). The lines are strong, but the meaning of the title is that dreams go wrong. Do these two elements go together well?<br/> <br/>I like this entry and would have voted it a 6. Why not higher? It''s too dark for a fantasy image.</td>', '***greetings from the critique club*** (I''m writing this before I read the comments and score...) These are thoughts as they occur to me. #1 Good strong lines. Is this wise given the theme of fantasy?#2 The overall darkness does not lend itself to a fantastic approach unless you wish to appear devilish.#3 The vinegetting is unfortunate.#4 More toning would fit the DPC style better: blacker darks and brighter whites.#5 Great lines (did I already say that?). The lines are strong, but the meaning of the title is that dreams go wrong. Do these two elements go together well? I like this entry and would have voted it a 6. Why not higher? It''s too dark for a fantasy image.', '2006-02-03 12:18:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118243, 284441, 37430, '<td valign="top">Hi Alan,<br/> <br/>Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>My standard text :)<br/>-------------------<br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. I hope following comments of mine are helpful to you.<br/> <br/>Composition :<br/>-------------<br/> <br/>Good composition and a funny picture to make anyone laugh. But as many other comments say - this could have been fit for wildlife challenge. I might think of my boss like this (and I do!) but I dont "fantasize" him (might have been different story if it was "her" ;)). <br/> <br/>Rest said and done, this is great capture (if theme of challenge is ignored)<br/> <br/>Camera Work :<br/>---------------<br/> <br/>Camera work is good. Image seems to be properly exposed. But a deeper DOF might have been helpful in keeping the beak in focus. Overall a decent photo ... technically.<br/> <br/>Post-processing :<br/>-----------------<br/> <br/>Okay ...now this needed some serious work on contrast. The image seems to be little flat. Looking at difference between shades of foreground subject and almost even background, proper use of "curves" would have done wonders. Some sharpening and saturation "could" have helped. Also you do not seem to be using maximum possible size limit for the file. Bigger size is normally less jpeg compression and thus better quality.<br/> <br/>Summary :<br/>----------<br/> <br/>Overall an above-average composition. I did not voted for this challenge but I would have also given something between 5-6 which is in line with average score. If this image is properly processed it can really be great "wildlife" picture. And off-course, shorter titles always have stronger impact!<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT)<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/>-Tej</td>', 'Hi Alan, Greetings from the Critique Club! My standard text :)-------------------I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. I hope following comments of mine are helpful to you. Composition :------------- Good composition and a funny picture to make anyone laugh. But as many other comments say - this could have been fit for wildlife challenge. I might think of my boss like this (and I do!) but I dont "fantasize" him (might have been different story if it was "her" ;)).  Rest said and done, this is great capture (if theme of challenge is ignored) Camera Work :--------------- Camera work is good. Image seems to be properly exposed. But a deeper DOF might have been helpful in keeping the beak in focus. Overall a decent photo ... technically. Post-processing :----------------- Okay ...now this needed some serious work on contrast. The image seems to be little flat. Looking at difference between shades of foreground subject and almost even background, proper use of "curves" would have done wonders. Some sharpening and saturation "could" have helped. Also you do not seem to be using maximum possible size limit for the file. Bigger size is normally less jpeg compression and thus better quality. Summary :---------- Overall an above-average composition. I did not voted for this challenge but I would have also given something between 5-6 which is in line with average score. If this image is properly processed it can really be great "wildlife" picture. And off-course, shorter titles always have stronger impact! If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT) Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-02-03 12:25:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118281, 282288, 37430, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>My standard text :)<br/>-------------------<br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. I hope following comments of mine are helpful to you.<br/> <br/>Composition :<br/>-------------<br/>Good fit for challenge, for sure. But somehow does not make a very "interesting" composition. <br/> <br/>Camera Work :<br/>-------------<br/>I really cannot make out much on camera work as post-processing has blurred most of the background, but sharp focus on dollars indicate that was good "quality" picture ... technically.<br/> <br/>Post-processing :<br/>-----------------<br/>Like most of the comment point out, the backgound is "too" blurred. You are right in saying that it lets you focus on subject, but in this case background (printer) also was significent part of overall theme. I feel shallow DOF over blurring would have better impact.<br/> <br/>Summary :<br/>----------<br/> <br/>Overall an above-average composition. I did not voted for this challenge but I would have also given something between 5-6 which is in line with average score. Better post-processing would have made this even better.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT)<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/>-Tej</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! My standard text :)-------------------I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. I hope following comments of mine are helpful to you. Composition :-------------Good fit for challenge, for sure. But somehow does not make a very "interesting" composition.  Camera Work :-------------I really cannot make out much on camera work as post-processing has blurred most of the background, but sharp focus on dollars indicate that was good "quality" picture ... technically. Post-processing :-----------------Like most of the comment point out, the backgound is "too" blurred. You are right in saying that it lets you focus on subject, but in this case background (printer) also was significent part of overall theme. I feel shallow DOF over blurring would have better impact. Summary :---------- Overall an above-average composition. I did not voted for this challenge but I would have also given something between 5-6 which is in line with average score. Better post-processing would have made this even better. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email, PM or IM (1530GMT-2330GMT) Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-02-03 12:35:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118305, 284728, 9073, '<td valign="top">***greetings from the critique club***<br/> <br/>(I''m writing these comments before reading the score and the other comments.)<br/> <br/>I find the image quite interesting and reasonably well done. I don''t, however, find it that compelling and would have given it a 5 in the challenge. I''ll explain why.<br/> <br/>For an image to appeal to me, I need a number of factors to work in coordination. The first is theme. The second is content, and the third is technique. Of course, there is a clear overlap between these factors, they can be discussed independantly.<br/> <br/>The challenge theme is fantasy. Beyond your title, I can''t see much evidence of that, sorry. I see well-carved engravings which might be enstrued as fantastic, or as magical instructions. I can''t see, however, any evidence that they should be. A single element photograph requires that element to be very strong. Your subject is strong but not strong enough to win at DPC, I fear. If the engravings had some encodable text, you would find some audience.<br/> <br/>The contents of the photo are the most important aspect of photography, an understatement perhaps. What do you have in this frame?<br/> <br/>You have a light reflection at the top, a tomb (?) inscription running from left top to bottom, an amber brick section on the bottom right and a shadow cutting into the tomb. <br/> <br/>How do these elements combine? I like the intersection of the shadows on the stone. They add a mystique. The black section on the right is very large. I wonder if it helps the photo?<br/> <br/>Overall, the technique is good, but the subject and elements are not that special. I''d give the photo a solid 5.<br/> <br/></td>', '***greetings from the critique club*** (I''m writing these comments before reading the score and the other comments.) I find the image quite interesting and reasonably well done. I don''t, however, find it that compelling and would have given it a 5 in the challenge. I''ll explain why. For an image to appeal to me, I need a number of factors to work in coordination. The first is theme. The second is content, and the third is technique. Of course, there is a clear overlap between these factors, they can be discussed independantly. The challenge theme is fantasy. Beyond your title, I can''t see much evidence of that, sorry. I see well-carved engravings which might be enstrued as fantastic, or as magical instructions. I can''t see, however, any evidence that they should be. A single element photograph requires that element to be very strong. Your subject is strong but not strong enough to win at DPC, I fear. If the engravings had some encodable text, you would find some audience. The contents of the photo are the most important aspect of photography, an understatement perhaps. What do you have in this frame? You have a light reflection at the top, a tomb (?) inscription running from left top to bottom, an amber brick section on the bottom right and a shadow cutting into the tomb.  How do these elements combine? I like the intersection of the shadows on the stone. They add a mystique. The black section on the right is very large. I wonder if it helps the photo? Overall, the technique is good, but the subject and elements are not that special. I''d give the photo a solid 5. ', '2006-02-03 12:42:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118582, 284578, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Congratulations on your splendid finish in the Fantasy Challenge.  Since you scored so well, it would be redundant of me to dwell on your image and tell you the same things your many commentors already have.<br/> <br/>So, I will move on to the next image in the queue, but not before telling you that since you are learning at warp speed, (from 5.2 to 7.2 in 5 Challenges) why not think about coming on board the Critique Club!  Your expertise would be welcome.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Congratulations on your splendid finish in the Fantasy Challenge.  Since you scored so well, it would be redundant of me to dwell on your image and tell you the same things your many commentors already have. So, I will move on to the next image in the queue, but not before telling you that since you are learning at warp speed, (from 5.2 to 7.2 in 5 Challenges) why not think about coming on board the Critique Club!  Your expertise would be welcome.', '2006-02-03 14:10:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2118638, 284508, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>You have such a pretty scene here, and it does meet the Challenge.  Unfortunately, it didn''t score very well, and you have some clues as perhaps why it didn''t from your commentors.<br/> <br/>The cloudy day obscured the bright and vibrant colors that exist in the ground and trees and the mist concealed the mountain.  When viewers cannot find a strong focal point, they tend to move on without looking for the interesting details that may lurk within.  <br/> <br/>Since this was an Advance Editing Challenge, you might consider the use of Levels and Curves and some of the other PS goodies to make the colors pop.  Another thought would be to crop drastically both up and most of the right side, to concentrate only on that mystical mountain with just a touch of treeline to indicate ground.  <br/> <br/>Obviously, these are just thoughts.  Your portfolio tells me you are a good photographer and I expect your next Challenge could be a winner!<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  You have such a pretty scene here, and it does meet the Challenge.  Unfortunately, it didn''t score very well, and you have some clues as perhaps why it didn''t from your commentors. The cloudy day obscured the bright and vibrant colors that exist in the ground and trees and the mist concealed the mountain.  When viewers cannot find a strong focal point, they tend to move on without looking for the interesting details that may lurk within.   Since this was an Advance Editing Challenge, you might consider the use of Levels and Curves and some of the other PS goodies to make the colors pop.  Another thought would be to crop drastically both up and most of the right side, to concentrate only on that mystical mountain with just a touch of treeline to indicate ground.   Obviously, these are just thoughts.  Your portfolio tells me you are a good photographer and I expect your next Challenge could be a winner! Alice', '2006-02-03 14:22:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2119452, 284543, 42013, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First Impression: nice silhouette against a pretty sky.<br/> <br/>Composition: You did a nice job on this picture with the placement of the bird. the close cropping really steers the viewers focus to the bird.  That said, personally I think a little more space on the right, and possibly the top would strengthen the image.<br/> <br/>Technical and PP: Exposure and colors look good.  One commentator mentioned a little fringing around the silhouette which is noticable.  I find this aspect hardly distracting to my eye because it is so slight.  the small greenish cloud right behind the birds head is a little bit of a distraction.  Advanced editing may have let you clone it out.??  The vignetting works well with this picture.  Did you do some selective blurring on the BG?<br/> <br/>Challenge results: 5.5xx is respectable. Maybe it didnt have that "hit the voter in the head" fantasy angle.  Keep up the good work. You have some nice pics in your portfolio.  I especially like your tribute entry.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me if any questions.<br/> <br/>mark</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club First Impression: nice silhouette against a pretty sky. Composition: You did a nice job on this picture with the placement of the bird. the close cropping really steers the viewers focus to the bird.  That said, personally I think a little more space on the right, and possibly the top would strengthen the image. Technical and PP: Exposure and colors look good.  One commentator mentioned a little fringing around the silhouette which is noticable.  I find this aspect hardly distracting to my eye because it is so slight.  the small greenish cloud right behind the birds head is a little bit of a distraction.  Advanced editing may have let you clone it out.??  The vignetting works well with this picture.  Did you do some selective blurring on the BG? Challenge results: 5.5xx is respectable. Maybe it didnt have that "hit the voter in the head" fantasy angle.  Keep up the good work. You have some nice pics in your portfolio.  I especially like your tribute entry. Feel free to PM me if any questions. mark', '2006-02-03 18:20:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2119568, 284686, 49007, '<td valign="top">**** Greeting from the Critique Club ****<br/> <br/>Challenge<br/>- Relevant to the Challenge? Yes<br/>- Is subject unique (vs. unoriginal or rehashed)? Yes very creative.<br/> <br/>Compostion<br/>- Good or Bad? How can it be fixed? A several people mention compositionally it would be better if the Mermaid were looking into the frame and not out of the frame. Reposition the camera so the she is on the right side of the frame, the rock is in the middle of the frame and the sun is in the top left. The sun in the center tends to dominate, taking away from the Mermaid as the focus.<br/>- Good use of Depth of Field? Yes<br/>- Good focus? Yes<br/> <br/>Lighting <br/>- Good use of light? Unfortunately, the foreground lighting (or flash) is over the top, making the rock seem like it''s glowing, hence the comments about the fake look. It almost looks like a studio shot, except you''d never get the halo of backlit hair (very nice) like this if it were studio.<br/> <br/>Aesthetics/Artistic Appeal:<br/>- Colors and Contrast The colors in the sky, water, and in the Mermaids ''scales'' are excellent.<br/>- What is my reaction or feelings? Very artistic. You have quite an interesting portfolio. I''m keeping my eye on you. ;)</td>', '**** Greeting from the Critique Club **** Challenge- Relevant to the Challenge? Yes- Is subject unique (vs. unoriginal or rehashed)? Yes very creative. Compostion- Good or Bad? How can it be fixed? A several people mention compositionally it would be better if the Mermaid were looking into the frame and not out of the frame. Reposition the camera so the she is on the right side of the frame, the rock is in the middle of the frame and the sun is in the top left. The sun in the center tends to dominate, taking away from the Mermaid as the focus.- Good use of Depth of Field? Yes- Good focus? Yes Lighting - Good use of light? Unfortunately, the foreground lighting (or flash) is over the top, making the rock seem like it''s glowing, hence the comments about the fake look. It almost looks like a studio shot, except you''d never get the halo of backlit hair (very nice) like this if it were studio. Aesthetics/Artistic Appeal:- Colors and Contrast The colors in the sky, water, and in the Mermaids ''scales'' are excellent.- What is my reaction or feelings? Very artistic. You have quite an interesting portfolio. I''m keeping my eye on you. ;)', '2006-02-03 18:52:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(2119686, 282913, 24940, '<td valign="top">Hey Dave, this is really a good photograph.  As agenkin says and I paraphrase, the scores you get say more about the audience than your picture.  Different strokes, as they say.  Personally, I like it.  Nice job!</td>', 'Hey Dave, this is really a good photograph.  As agenkin says and I paraphrase, the scores you get say more about the audience than your picture.  Different strokes, as they say.  Personally, I like it.  Nice job!', '2006-02-03 19:16:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(2119757, 284603, 19672, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club ::: </i></b>[The Saj]<br/> <br/><b>First Impressions:</b>  Gorgeous....and that''s why I gave it a 9 in voting.<br/> <br/>-------------------------------------------------------------<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b>  Composition is very strong. And although I like outtake too, I do believe this has the stronger composition, albeit a little more traditional.  The lighthouse is well placed, as is the strong build of rock to the right. Furthermore, the composition of the entry allows for the inlet of the water to be nicely captured.<br/> <br/>The one minor compositional note is perhaps an ever so slight feel of leaning on the part of the lighthouse itself. This may simply be minor lense distortion. I am not sure.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> An excellent choice of subject.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour, focus, and light):</b> The colors are vibrant and bleak, with a wintry feel. The near IR exposure added an extra flair.   The focus is quite sharp with very strong detail and texture.  The lighting is excellent. Of note, the left side of the lighthouse might be just a touch over-exposed.<br/> <br/><b>Creativity:</b> Creative application of an uncommon technique on an excellent subject.<br/> <br/>-------------------------------------------------------------<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> What can I say....I wish it were mine.  Actually, if I might, the skies transition? almost feels a bit overdone and slightly unnatural. (Even if it it might be natural.)   Other than that, this is a magnificant shot and one you should consider making available as a print.<br/> <br/><b>To improve?:</b>  Perhaps a slight softening on the processing of the sky transition. Some minor lense distortion adjustment. And perhaps a little more processing on the light/dark transition of the lighthouse.<br/> <br/>Beautiful job...bravo...you have done quite well. It wasn''t a ribbon winner but it''s a brilliant shot. This was actually one of my personal favorites from this challenge. <br/> <br/>-------------------------------------------------------------<br/> <br/>It is my hope that these insights are helpful, and constructive. If you have any questions regarding this critique, please feel free to PM me. Also feel free to PM me with any feedback on this critique. And please remember to mark it "Helpful" if you found it so.<br/> <br/>- Jason "The Saj"<br/> <br/></td>', '::: Critique Club ::: [The Saj] First Impressions:  Gorgeous....and that''s why I gave it a 9 in voting. ------------------------------------------------------------- Composition:  Composition is very strong. And although I like outtake too, I do believe this has the stronger composition, albeit a little more traditional.  The lighthouse is well placed, as is the strong build of rock to the right. Furthermore, the composition of the entry allows for the inlet of the water to be nicely captured. The one minor compositional note is perhaps an ever so slight feel of leaning on the part of the lighthouse itself. This may simply be minor lense distortion. I am not sure. Subject: An excellent choice of subject. Technical (Colour, focus, and light): The colors are vibrant and bleak, with a wintry feel. The near IR exposure added an extra flair.   The focus is quite sharp with very strong detail and texture.  The lighting is excellent. Of note, the left side of the lighthouse might be just a touch over-exposed. Creativity: Creative application of an uncommon technique on an excellent subject. ------------------------------------------------------------- Summary: What can I say....I wish it were mine.  Actually, if I might, the skies transition? almost feels a bit overdone and slightly unnatural. (Even if it it might be natural.)   Other than that, this is a magnificant shot and one you should consider making available as a print. To improve?:  Perhaps a slight softening on the processing of the sky transition. Some minor lense distortion adjustment. And perhaps a little more processing on the light/dark transition of the lighthouse. Beautiful job...bravo...you have done quite well. It wasn''t a ribbon winner but it''s a brilliant shot. This was actually one of my personal favorites from this challenge.  ------------------------------------------------------------- It is my hope that these insights are helpful, and constructive. If you have any questions regarding this critique, please feel free to PM me. Also feel free to PM me with any feedback on this critique. And please remember to mark it "Helpful" if you found it so. - Jason "The Saj" ', '2006-02-03 19:38:40.000000', NULL, 0),
